{"completed": true, "response": "<escape>{\n  \"report_supported\": \"partly\",\n  \"judge_supported\": \"partly\",\n  \"report_quotes\": [\n    \"Notably, none of the DBS stimulation modes reached statistical significance for the FOG-Q score outcome, the ranking suggests that certain DBS interventions may still confer potential improvements in FOG-Q scores.\",\n    \"The results indicate that the 95% CIs for all interventions cross zero, and none of the p-values reached statistical significance (p > 0.05), suggesting that no DBS intervention demonstrated a reliable statistical effect in improving FOG-Q scores; although all SMD values were negative and hinted at slight improvements, the overall strength of evidence remained limited.\",\n    \"Notably, none of the DBS modalities yielded statistically significant improvements in Freezing of Gait (FOG) as measured by the FOG-Q, with all confidence intervals crossing zero. This finding reflects the multifactorial and episodic nature of FOG (Ehgoetz Martens et al., 2018; Weiss et al., 2020), which is likely mediated by extranigral circuits such as the pedunculopontine nucleus and mesencephalic locomotor region (Wojcik et al., 2025; Factor et al., 2025; Camastra et al., 2025), a meta-analyses (Schlenstedt et al., 2017) suggest that STN-DBS improves gait and FOG in the medication-off/stimulation-on state, suggesting that the observed lack of significant effect in this study may be influenced by variations in study design, stimulation parameters, or patient characteristics.\"\n  ],\n  \"judge_quotes\": [\n    \"Twenty-five RCTs were included, involving 324 patients in intervention groups and 324 in control groups.\",\n    \"The network evidence diagram (Figure 4A) depicts that the 10 included studies collectively evaluated a total of 11 distinct stimulation modalities: 60 Hz-STN-DBS, spDBS, 80 Hz-STN-DBS, right STN-DBS, left STN-DBS, IL-IL-DBS, TBS-DBS, STN + SNr LF-DBS, STN + SNr HF-DBS, PPNa-DBS, and STN-DBS.\",\n    \"The overall estimates indicate that, compared with the control group, none of the DBS interventions yielded statistically significant improvements in FOG-Q scores.\",\n    \"To systematically compare and rank the relative efficacy of different types of deep brain stimulation (DBS) for gait disorders in patients with Parkinson’s disease (PD), with particular emphasis on three outcomes: motor function (MDS-UPDRS III), Freezing of Gait Questionnaire (FOG-Q), and normal gait velocity (cm/s).\",\n    \"Included: Other DBS types, sham stimulation, off DBS. Head-to-head comparisons of different DBS targets (e.g., STN vs. GPi, STN vs. aDBS, GPi vs. PPN) were eligible, allowing evaluation of differential effects on gait outcomes.\",\n    \"Existing studies on STN-DBS and GPi-DBS largely address overall motor symptoms and are limited by small samples, heterogeneous designs, and reliance on pairwise analyses, leaving the comparative effects of different DBS modalities on gait disorders insufficiently defined (Deuschl et al., 2022; Fan et al., 2021; Lin et al., 2021).\"\n  ],\n  \"why\": \"报告的说法部分有依据，但将结果描述为方向冲突不准确。原文确实在Discussion中将本研究FOG-Q未达到统计学显著性，与既往Meta分析认为STN-DBS能够改善步态和FOG的结论进行了比较。然而，原文同时明确指出，FOG-Q分析中所有SMD值均为负值，提示轻微改善（all SMD values were negative and hinted at slight improvements）。因此，本研究并未显示与既往研究相反的效应方向，而是显示改善方向的估计未达到统计学显著性。无统计学显著性不等于效应方向相反，这是报告缺少的关键限定。指控者关于研究数量及FOG-Q统计结果的描述有明确依据：全文纳入25项RCT，但FOG-Q网络分析仅纳入10项研究，且11种DBS模式均未达到统计学显著性。不过，关于估计目标的描述需要适当限定。本文的主要研究目标确实是比较和排序不同DBS模式的相对疗效，比较网络包括其他DBS模式、sham及DBS-off。但原文也明确报告了各DBS模式相对于control group的FOG-Q效应估计。因此，不能简单认为本文完全不估计DBS相对于对照的治疗效应。此外，全文没有详细说明被比较的另一项Meta分析的具体估计量、比较对象及分析方法，因而仅凭本文不能完全确认两项研究估计的治疗效应必然不同。综合而言，报告对结果差异的比较有文本依据，但将其定性为方向冲突缺乏依据；指控者的核心统计描述正确，其关于估计目标不同的判断大体合理，但不能将其理解为本文完全没有估计相对于对照组的治疗效果。\"\n}</escape>", "metadata": {"session_handle": "e15b6a55-78e8-49b2-894c-fe73e5cfc35c", "session_revision": 0, "session_ttl_max_hours": 6, "job_directory": "D:\\musicclaude\\gpt2agent-safechain\\runs\\gpt4\\pro-bridge-20261114T190018832004Z", "elapsed_seconds": 99.547, "completed_utc": "2026-11-14T19:01:58.396456+00:00", "model": "gpt-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "max", "temporary": true, "unpersonalized": true, "generation_policy_verified": true, "request_policy": {"model": "gpt-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "max", "history_and_training_disabled": true, "temporary_chat_requests_personalization": false, "is_do_not_remember": false, "continuation": false, "unpersonalized_inherited_verified": false, "body_sha256": "472ce9c4f6f1676869f159958390980e4e2d0f08aab37ca7708262c2b7b1c0e0", "attachment_count": 1}, "stream_completion": {"sse_done": true, "message_completed": true, "independent_terminal_verified": true}, "profile": "Nozomi Kasaki / gpt4", "account_sha256": "bbd4fd636d4b6714d2b42b89e341bdb5bbe7c34f7d141aec76b444be8343fe54", "transport": "probridge-pure-http", "resume_requests": 0, "topic_connections": 0, "browser_started": false, "Docker_used": false, "automatic_retry": false, "initializer_pss_limit_mib": "not_applicable_no_browser", "attachment_count": 1, "code_sha256": {"tools\\chatgpt_web\\gpt3_cli.py": "96a4cdb6cd5d982f4d1de5b6ba8fae0cf23ee44aa3e41d7fdf6968e3fb590e48", "tools\\chatgpt_web\\gpt3_sessions.py": "83a3ed4ae1efa9f1a2b8ee3f144cc0dc9f668f995a33cc4222308cb3fa31f683", "tools\\chatgpt_web\\gpt3_attachments.py": "eedfa23885000025700f6306b1d1f4582896a7370734be506c156dee532fd7d7"}}, "screenshot_evidence": {"complete": true, "kind": "offline_actual_HTTP_response", "directory": "D:\\musicclaude\\gpt2agent-safechain\\runs\\gpt4\\pro-bridge-20261114T190018832004Z", "files": ["proof-offline.png"], "sha256": {"proof-offline.png": "29708834c05ca915005d2290f1308be75b73289fa050ff73edb8c1f508bba268"}}}
